-- Which professions want an item as a crafting reagent, one bit each.
--
-- GENERATED by Scripts/build_reagent_data.py from the recipe captures under
-- docs/recipes/. Do not edit by hand: tests/python/test_reagent_data.py re-runs
-- the generator and compares, so a hand edit fails the suite rather than
-- surviving in it.
--
-- Every entry is the game client walking a profession's own recipe list, so the
-- attribution is exact for what the client showed. Ten professions have a
-- recipe list. Herbalism, Skinning, Fishing and Archaeology do not, and First
-- Aid no longer exists in retail -- those five have no entries at all, and that
-- is the right answer rather than a gap: nothing is crafted from them, so
-- nothing can be their reagent.
--
-- ABSENCE MEANS "NOT KNOWN", NEVER "NOBODY WANTS THIS". These are one client at
-- one build. A recipe a later patch adds is missing until the captures are
-- retaken, and so is every reagent only that recipe consumes. A consumer
-- reading nil as a no would vendor exactly the reagents this exists to protect.
-- Fall through to your own rules instead.
--
-- 2833 reagents across 10 professions, from 10308 recipes.

---@class BitForge.Core
local ns = select(2, ...)

---@type BitForge.Core.Enum
local enum = ns.enum

-- The client build every capture was taken against. model.IsReagentDataStale
